language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ giant_cat_army: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Start with [0], then build a list by using one of three operations: adding 5, adding 7, or
$ taking the square root. The game is complete when the list has 2, 10 and 14 appearing in that
$ order (other numbers may lie between them). All the elements are distinct, all are at most 60,
$ and all are integers. The list starts with 0, has length 24 and ends with 14.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/giant_cat_army.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ the number of elements of the list
letting list_len = 24
$ every element is at most this
letting max_value = 60

$ x[i]: the i-th number of the list, counting from 1; the list starts with 0 and has list_len numbers
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..list_len)] of int(0..max_value)

such that
$ the list starts with 0
x[1] = 0,
$ the list ends with 14
x[list_len] = 14,
$ all the numbers in the list are different
allDiff(x),
$ each step adds 5, adds 7, or takes the square root (the next number is the square root of the previous one, so it must be an exact integer)
forAll i : int(1..list_len-1) . ((x[i+1] = x[i] + 5) \/ (x[i+1] = x[i] + 7) \/ (x[i+1] * x[i+1] = x[i])),
$ 2, 10 and 14 appear in that order (other numbers may lie between them); 14 is the last element, so it is enough that 2 comes before 10
exists i : int(1..list_len-1) . exists j : int(1..list_len-1) . (i < j /\ x[i] = 2 /\ x[j] = 10)
